    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nAudi Is Making Just 182 Nuvolari Supercars for the U.S. Market, It Asks $700,000 for One\n59 photos\nPhoto: Audi\nGerman carmaker Audi announced this week that it will produce a limited number of the Nuvolari supercar and send just 182 of them to the U.S. in the first half of 2028. On top of being so few of them, there will be so many customization options that they'll likely cause quite the headaches for anyone who has the money and intention to buy one.\nThe number represents a sizeable chunk of the total of 499 units that are being made for the global market. For North America, the German carmaker announced a starting price of $700,000, not including destination, taxes, title, other options, and dealer charges.\nPresented back in early summer, the road-going supercar made its American debut during the Monterey Car Week back in August. It was received with a lot of praise, not only on account of the way it looks, but also because it represents the most powerful and fastest production Audi in history, something that is bound to attract the attention of rich buyers.\nThe shape of the car, looking like some sort of alien spaceship descended to the ground, owes part of its looks to the carbon fiber bodywork and the active aerodynamics fitted all over, including a three-stage deployable rear wing, a drag-reduction system, and other aerodynamic elements that conspire to generate some 800 pounds (400 kg) of downforce.\nPhoto: Audi Built around the all-new Audi Space Frame, almost all of the exterior panels that come together to form the Nuvolari are made in carbon fiber, shaped with methods used in Formula 1, just like the DRS mentioned above. And underneath it all hide a number of mechanical solutions that have never before been used by the brand with four rings in this way.\nAnd it all starts with the powertrain, an unforgiving combination between an internal combustion engine and electric motors. A hybrid by trade, the supercar employs a high-revving V8 biturbo engine and three electric axial flux motors (two on the front and one on the rear axle) to develop a combined 987 horsepower (U.S.-spec).\nOf that, 789 hp, achieved from 9,000 – 9,750 rpm, come from the ICE, and an extra 198 hp from the electric motors. The troop allows the Nuvolari to hit 60 mph from a perfect standstill in 2.5 seconds, on to a top speed of 217 mph (349 kph).\nA new brake-by-wire technology backed by ceramic disks and a series of launch control strategies help the driver harness the immense power levels, while a brand-new, never-before-used quattro predictive ride is on deck to make sure none of that goes to waste.\nPhoto: Audi The quattro system makes use of data generated by an undisclosed number of sensors to analyze things like grip levels, steering inputs, acceleration, and vehicle behavior, and, based on that, provide the proper torque distribution, braking interventions, and aerodynamic balance. To do that, the system also ties into a four-way adjustable suspension setup.\nAudi is sending not only the above to the American market, but also a lot of customization choices that are bound to make every single one of the 182 American examples (and probably the global ones as well) unique in its own way.\nFor the exterior, for instance, the Germans let people choose from more than 60 colors (50 gloss paints and around 10 matte ones), and match that with one of 13 brake caliper colors. On top of that, there will also be an undisclosed number of Audi Exclusive hues. As a side note, Audi also offers the choice of going for a fully exposed carbon fiber exterior.\nPhoto: Audi Things don't get easier when it comes to the inside either. Offering just two undisclosed materials to choose from, the Nuvolari throws on the table three primary Dinamica colors and no less than 33 leather color options, a dozen seat belt colors, and a couple of switchgear colors. Finally, the company also talks about fully customized Audi Exclusive paints and interior options, but it does not dive into the details of what that means.\nThe carmaker is already accepting orders for the insane vehicle, but it only does this at authorized Audi dealerships throughout the country, and it won't do that forever – there is no mention of when the books to reserve a Nuvolari close, but it will probably be soon. As said, deliveries are expected to begin in 2028, which is about one year after the first examples will be delivered to customers in the rest of the world.\nAudi sees the extreme ride as a first step to a new direction in car design and engineering, so it's very likely more things such as this will follow in the not-so-distant future. And however these new things will manifest themselves, they will likely be even more extreme, as Audi has already committed to bringing the innovations of Formula 1 to the road in a constant manner.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAudi Nuvolari Audi supercar\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT Challenger Hellcat Races Shelby GT500 in 1,400-HP Dog Fight in Europe, the Red Fat American Wins",
